{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"PHASE 1 ROOT CAUSE (proven offline, no live Jira mutation) — rebar-debug.\n\nMECHANISM. `_poll_until_visible` (tests/integration/test_reconcile_live_e2e.py:419-455) polls\nJira by calling `client.search_issues(...)` with two CONSTANT JQL strings — `key = \"<KEY>\"` and\n`summary ~ \"<summary>\"` — on a SINGLE `AcliClient` instance. `AcliClient.search_issues`\n(src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira/acli.py:413-453) memoizes results per-JQL in an\nunbounded, never-invalidated `self._search_cache`, and it caches NEGATIVE (empty) results too:\n\n    if jql not in self._search_cache:   # acli.py:417\n        ... run acli ...\n        self._search_cache[jql] = all_issues   # acli.py:451\n\nSo only the FIRST poll iteration reaches Jira. Every later iteration — and the post-loop final\n`_issue_exists` at line 455 — is a cache hit that re-reads the first, empty answer. The 120s\n\"budget\" with 1/2/4/8s exponential backoff is a loop that sleeps for two minutes and never asks\nJira again. The helper is a SINGLE-SHOT check wearing a retry loop's clothes: it can only pass if\nJira's index has already converged at the instant of the very first query, and once that first\nquery misses, failure is deterministic regardless of budget, backoff, or env override.\n\nDISCRIMINATING EXPERIMENT (offline, deterministic, no network). Drove the real `AcliClient`\nthrough `_poll_until_visible` with an injected simulated clock, stubbing only `AcliClient._run`\n(the subprocess seam) to model an index converging at t=60s — HALF the 120s budget:\n\n  RESULT found                  : False\n  simulated clock at exit       : t=127.0s   (full budget burned)\n  acli searches actually issued : 2  — both at t=0.0s\n      t=0.0s  key = \"REB-3160\"\n      t=0.0s  summary ~ \"DELETE-ME delete-permission probe\"\n  populated cache keys          : ['key = \"REB-3160\"', 'summary ~ \"DELETE-ME ...\"']\n\nPredicted-if-cache-poisoning: False, exactly 2 searches. Predicted-if-budget-too-short: True at\nt=60s, ~34 searches. Observed = the former. \"Budget too short\", \"backoff shape\", \"Jira rate\nlimiting\", and \"acli subprocess timeout under load\" are all REJECTED as the mechanism: the\nfailure reproduces with zero network, zero load, and a budget twice the convergence time.\n\nCONTROL — why the existing unit tests are green. The same scenario against this module's own\n`_EventuallyVisibleClient` fake (line ~473) returns True at t=63s. That fake has no\n`_search_cache` (`hasattr(fake, \"_search_cache\")` is False), so it cannot express the collaborator\nbehaviour that breaks the poll. The three simulated-clock tests added by the previous fix are\ntherefore structurally incapable of detecting this defect.\n\nWHY THE PREVIOUS FIX DID NOT FIX IT. Commit 82dcb2a189 (\"d30c: harden live-E2E index-visibility\npoll vs Jira lag\") tuned budget 30s->120s, flat-2s -> exponential backoff, and added the\nfake-based tests. Every one of those knobs is downstream of a query that is never issued. The\nthree clean serial runs recorded afterwards were coincidence — runs where the first query happened\nto land after the index converged — not evidence the mechanism was addressed. The 2026-08-18 xdist\nregression (REB-3160) is not a new defect: it is the original defect, which was never fixed.\n\nWHY IT LOOKS LOAD-DEPENDENT. Load does not cause the failure; it only changes the probability that\nthe FIRST query misses. In isolation on an idle Jira the first query often lands after convergence\n(pass). Under full-live-group load, and more so under a full-suite xdist run, create->first-query\nlatency and index lag both shift so the first query misses — and from that instant the outcome is\nsealed. This is why the bug presented as \"flaky under load\" while being a hard logic defect.\n\nThe cache is NOT primed by an earlier test: the client is constructed fresh inside the test\n(line ~343) and the JQL embeds the just-created key. The poisoning is self-inflicted within the\npoll itself.\n\nPRIOR ART / THE PROJECT ALREADY KNOWS THIS RULE. `access_check.py:152-158` runs its own JQL retry\nloop and explicitly evicts the cache entry before EVERY attempt:\n\n    for attempt in range(JQL_RETRY_COUNT):\n        cache = getattr(client, \"_search_cache\", None)\n        if isinstance(cache, dict):\n            cache.pop(jql, None)\n        results = client.search_issues(jql)\n\nThat is the same rule the E2E poll is missing — a parity gap, not a novel discovery. Relatedly,\n`_verify_created_issue` (adapters/jira/acli_cli_ops.py:46-49) documents that it deliberately uses a\n\"direct REST GET (immediately consistent) instead of JQL search, which is subject to Jira Cloud's\neventual-consistency index lag\" — the codebase's own statement that JQL search is the lag-prone\npath this poll was supposed to absorb.\n\nDEFECT LEGITIMACY: defect. The behaviour violates (a) the helper's own documented contract —\n\"Returns True as soon as either sees it\", \"a slow index is waited out patiently\" (docstring,\nline ~430); (b) the existing test `test_poll_until_visible_waits_out_slow_index_convergence`, whose\nmessage \"index-visibility poll gave up before eventual convergence at t=60s\" encodes exactly the\nintent the real client defeats; and (c) this very ticket's prior closed Fixed: state for the same\nbehaviour. No budget was widened to reach this conclusion.\n\nNO LIVE JIRA WAS TOUCHED. All of the above is offline and deterministic. Phase 2 proceeds under\nRED->GREEN with a held-out oracle.\n\nSECOND DISCRIMINATING EXPERIMENT (independent investigator, offline). Same harness, but the\nstubbed `_run` RAISES `CalledProcessError` while t < 60s instead of returning an empty list.\nResult: found=True, 21 searches issued, converged at t=63s. The poll retries CORRECTLY on the one\ncondition it was not designed for (transport error — the exception escapes `search_issues` before\nthe cache store at acli.py:451 and is swallowed by `_issue_exists`), and never retries the one it\nWAS designed for (a successful-but-empty index answer, which the cache freezes). This asymmetry is\nthe cleanest possible kill for the acli-subprocess-timeout and Jira-rate-limit hypotheses: those\nsurface as exceptions, and the exception path provably polls for the full budget.\n\nTwo independent investigators (code-tracer lens and this orchestrator) converged on the identical\nmechanism and artifact from separate starting points.\n\nLATENT PRODUCTION EXPOSURE (out of scope here; ticket to be filed). The same unbounded, TTL-less\n`_search_cache` lives on the long-lived `AcliClient` instances built at\n`src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/runtime.py:145` and `adapters/jira/backend.py:359`. Any repeated\nJQL on a client that outlives one pagination sequence serves a permanently stale answer;\n`binding_recovery.py:342-346` counts `search_miss_count` across passes on the assumption each pass\nissues a fresh query."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787172244037812001,"uuid":"1d34b1ff-b66d-45c6-ab80-581995984f88"}